  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 220 PM MST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nGOES-19 satellite imagery shows mostly clear skies this afternoon \nafter dense fog this morning across northeast Colorado. Today and \nTuesday will be dry for the area and mild with ridging aloft. High \ntemperatures on the plains are forecast to be in the low to mid 60s. \nHigh-resolution guidance is in good agreement that a Denver Cyclone \nis likely to form on Tuesday. As a result, some locations on the \neastern plains could see wind gusts of 25-35 mph tomorrow afternoon. \nAs the week progresses, high temperatures on the plains will cool \ndown into the 50s. By late Tuesday evening into early Wednesday \nmorning, troughing should move into the state. This will open the \ndoor for snow chances in the high country. Most locations will only \nsee a dusting at most with limited moisture in place, but the Park \nRange and northern Gore Range could get up to 2 inches of snow given \nfavorable upslope.\n\nBy early Wednesday morning as any precipitation begins to wind down, \nthe ingredients for a Bora event could come together for our area. \nCold air advection, little wind shear, and strong near-westerly \ncross barrier flow is expected to develop with mixed layer winds \nreaching or even exceeding 50-60 kts through much of Wednesday \nmorning and into the early afternoon. On Wednesday morning, models \nshow northeast CO near the right exit region of a jet aloft as well \nas strong subsidence which would aid in increasing wind speeds. \nModel soundings from the latest RRFS runs have shown a stable layer \naround 650-700 mb, so this could be something to watch for in terms \nof any enhancement of winds. Some potential limiting factors include \nonly a very weak surface pressure gradient and the jet positioning. \nBy Wednesday afternoon, the jet could be overhead, which would \nlikely confine the strong wind gusts to far northern portions of our \narea and into Wyoming. \n\nREFS and NBM mean max wind gusts on Wednesday now show peak wind \ngusts of at least 60-65 mph for the northern Front Range mountains \nand 50-60 mph for the northern Front Range foothills. The high \nresolution guidance that has come in today is hinting at the \npotential for a few stronger wind gusts to make it down into the \ntypical windy areas in these setups along the Front Range such as \nthe Hwy 93 corridor as well as northern Weld County. This solution \nis still rather uncertain, but as of now we expect wind gusts to \nstay below high wind criteria for the Hwy 93 corridor. \n\nAs is typical in these setups, there could be some narrow channels \nof 30-40 mph wind gusts for the Denver metro if the ingredients can \ncome together. In general, confidence is increasing in the potential \nfor a high wind event for portions of the high country north of I-70 \nincluding the higher terrain of Larimer and Boulder counties on \nWednesday morning and potentially lasting into the afternoon. \nTherefore, we have issued a High Wind Watch for the northern\nFront Range foothills from 2am to 2pm Wednesday. The High Wind\nWatch could need to be expanded in future packages if confidence \nincreases in the peak wind gusts meeting criteria at the highest \nelevations.\n\nOn Thursday, we expect some weak ridging with near-average \ntemperatures. The next system looks to move in starting Friday, but \nshould progress slower than the early week system. Once again, \nthis system will favor the high country for any precipitation with\nsouthwest to west flow aloft. Precipitation chances will likely \ncontinue on Saturday. Ensemble guidance shows that the plains has \na very low chance for some light scattered rain from this system. \nAt this time, snow chances remain near zero for Denver through at \nleast this weekend. Therefore, we are nearly guaranteed to have a \ntop 5 latest first snow for Denver. With another strong jet aloft \nmoving through and a tightening pressure gradient, we could be in \nfor another period of stronger wind gusts Friday afternoon into \nSaturday. From Sunday onward, a more zonal pattern looks to be in \nplace with signs in ensemble guidance of near-average to below \naverage temperatures becoming more likely.",
